1
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:38,095
INTERNATIONAL SPACE STATION

2
00:02:38,175 --> 00:02:41,403
MARS PILGRIM 7 MISSION
DAY 1

3
00:02:59,465 --> 00:03:04,628
This is Dr Miranda North, Quarantine officer
aboard the International Space Station.

4
00:03:05,104 --> 00:03:08,506
Today the Pilgrim capsule with
it's valuable Mars soil samples

5
00:03:08,531 --> 00:03:11,906
completes it's 8 month
return from Mars.

6
00:03:12,799 --> 00:03:14,546
Preparing to receive these samples

7
00:03:14,571 --> 00:03:18,185
has been the purpose of our
mission these last few months.

8
00:03:18,903 --> 00:03:21,548
But the Pilgrim capsule is damaged.

9
00:03:22,079 --> 00:03:24,146
At 06:30 UTC

10
00:03:24,310 --> 00:03:27,582
Pilgrim was struck by debris
and veered off course.

11
00:03:27,761 --> 00:03:29,057
Rory Adams will space walk

12
00:03:29,082 --> 00:03:31,847
to establish visual contact
with the incoming space capsule

13
00:03:31,872 --> 00:03:34,611
and he will grab it
with the remote arm.

14
00:03:35,986 --> 00:03:39,362
We've been waiting a long
time for this sample

15
00:03:40,034 --> 00:03:43,122
and Rory is placing himself
at great risk to secure it.

16
00:03:43,727 --> 00:03:46,524
- Over and out.
- EMU report: 4.7 psi, pure O2.

17
00:03:46,672 --> 00:03:49,018
So how you gonna do it?
I wouldn't do it that way.

18
00:03:49,208 --> 00:03:51,036
- Dr Jordan.
- Faster, faster, faster...

19
00:03:51,061 --> 00:03:52,603
Nitrogen is zero.

20
00:03:52,675 --> 00:03:55,401
- Dr North? I'm in the airlock.
- Faster, faster.. come on.

21
00:03:55,485 --> 00:03:58,227
- Rory, slow to go fast.
- Or fast to go fast.

22
00:03:58,305 --> 00:04:00,003
- Deliberate.
- We're here to get fast.

23
00:04:00,028 --> 00:04:03,322
Remember, 8 billion people.
Rory, if you can't save it...

24
00:04:03,347 --> 00:04:05,115
Yeah, spoiler alert!
I'm fucking saving it!

25
00:04:05,146 --> 00:04:09,094
But if you can't, just push the capsule
into deep space, ok? Do you hear me?

26
00:04:09,172 --> 00:04:12,360
Can you get OCD, sorry,
CDC, out of my face?

27
00:04:12,439 --> 00:04:14,391
'Cause if I got a big operation,
I need to concentrate.

28
00:04:14,422 --> 00:04:16,079
- I just want you...
- I can't, I can't hear you...

29
00:04:16,125 --> 00:04:17,227
- I love you too.
- At all.

30
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:20,164
- EMU at 4.7 psi, pure O2.
- Where is the remote?

31
00:04:20,351 --> 00:04:23,082
- Here. The arm is yours, full manual.
- All right, here we go.

32
00:04:23,735 --> 00:04:26,184
- Careful with.. sorry, you know this.
- Yeah, I got it.

33
00:04:26,375 --> 00:04:28,532
- No more sorries.
- Miranda, to the cupola, please.

34
00:04:29,770 --> 00:04:30,262
Yep.

35
00:04:59,311 --> 00:05:02,119
- David, are you on your way?
- On my way.

36
00:05:02,767 --> 00:05:04,498
- Slow, Sho.
- Excuse me.

37
00:05:04,837 --> 00:05:06,477
How you say "breath" in Japanese?

38
00:05:07,557 --> 00:05:09,189
This was supposed to be a routine.

39
00:05:09,275 --> 00:05:12,972
The air lock is sealed.
I.. am.. walking.

40
00:05:16,511 --> 00:05:20,792
04:35 UTC. After striking
debris, Pilgrim non-responsive.

41
00:05:21,229 --> 00:05:23,384
Inbound on a collision
course with the ISS.

42
00:05:23,919 --> 00:05:26,855
Initiate emergency course
correction and manual retrieval.

43
00:05:27,578 --> 00:05:31,283
Cannon armed online.
Transferring control to wireless.

44
00:05:31,525 --> 00:05:33,737
Station to Control.
Initiating sequence.

45
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:34,977
Affirmative.

46
00:05:35,196 --> 00:05:37,736
- So, the committee?
- You let me worry about the committee.

47
00:05:38,159 --> 00:05:39,127
Where's Rory?

48
00:05:40,063 --> 00:05:43,558
- He is at risk out there.
- Easy folks, mics are live.

49
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,507
- Ready, Sho?
- Thrusters up.

50
00:05:51,916 --> 00:05:53,273
Holding in position.

51
00:05:54,117 --> 00:05:55,314
I have control of the arm.

52
00:06:32,982 --> 00:06:34,913
Your worry lines are showing.

53
00:06:35,529 --> 00:06:37,421
Yeah, well, I get paid by the line.

54
00:06:40,084 --> 00:06:42,593
- I have a good feeling about this.
- Yeah?

55
00:06:44,302 --> 00:06:47,320
- Rory is gonna deliver that sample.
- Right to your lab.

56
00:06:50,606 --> 00:06:52,326
Prepare for course correction.

57
00:06:54,119 --> 00:06:56,991
.0509 .076

58
00:06:57,420 --> 00:06:59,347
- Remember your training.
- We never trained for this!

59
00:06:59,690 --> 00:07:00,443
On my mark!

60
00:07:00,522 --> 00:07:01,123
5

61
00:07:01,147 --> 00:07:01,900
4

62
00:07:01,925 --> 00:07:02,636
3

63
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:03,417
2

64
00:07:03,441 --> 00:07:04,076
1

65
00:07:14,472 --> 00:07:19,907
- New capsule trajectory, negative 0.50.
- Proximity alert. Proximity alert.

66
00:07:20,063 --> 00:07:21,733
Ignore the proximity alert.

67
00:07:22,092 --> 00:07:24,725
New station trajectory: negative 0.32.

68
00:07:25,327 --> 00:07:27,609
The capsule will miss us, just.

69
00:07:28,131 --> 00:07:29,961
- Rory?
- I still don't reach.

70
00:07:31,782 --> 00:07:33,247
It's coming in shallow.

71
00:07:34,435 --> 00:07:36,869
Any moment now.
8, maybe 9 clock.

72
00:07:37,698 --> 00:07:39,437
I'm having fun.
You guys having fun?

73
00:07:39,633 --> 00:07:40,406
Rory..

74
00:07:40,955 --> 00:07:42,669
- if you miss...
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

75
00:07:42,810 --> 00:07:43,428
If...

76
00:07:44,225 --> 00:07:47,994
Just gonna skip off the atmosphere,
like a flat stone on water.

77
00:07:48,790 --> 00:07:51,503
Just fly off into nowhere,
no harm done.

78
00:07:52,940 --> 00:07:54,123
Okay.

79
00:07:54,832 --> 00:07:55,723
There!

80
00:07:56,589 --> 00:07:58,120
- There.
- I see it.

81
00:07:58,756 --> 00:08:00,969
All right. Here we go, here we go...
This is fun.

82
00:08:01,292 --> 00:08:04,321
- Rory, you have to move, it's gonna hit you.
- That's bad.

83
00:08:04,449 --> 00:08:06,069
Please Rory, move.

84
00:08:06,894 --> 00:08:10,442
Station to Control. Visual
confirmation of Mars Capsule Pilgrim.

85
00:08:10,763 --> 00:08:12,890
I used to play catcher,
but only in T-ball.

86
00:08:13,076 --> 00:08:14,253
- Rory?
- Not now.

87
00:08:15,075 --> 00:08:19,055
Not now. Come here, come here,
come on, come on...

88
00:08:23,747 --> 00:08:24,447
Rory?

89
00:08:26,162 --> 00:08:27,696
- Rory?
- Rory?

90
00:08:30,165 --> 00:08:31,212
He caught it!

91
00:08:35,648 --> 00:08:36,465
Hugh...

92
00:08:37,088 --> 00:08:38,606
Cue Instagram!

93
00:08:39,044 --> 00:08:40,298
I didn't wanna jinx it.

94
00:08:41,917 --> 00:08:45,226
- Point and shoot, motherfucker!
- Okay.

95
00:08:58,444 --> 00:09:01,689
Mars Capsule in lock position.

96
00:09:07,490 --> 00:09:08,673
I'm coming home.

97
00:09:11,271 --> 00:09:14,192
This is mission commander,
Ekaterina Golovkina.

98
00:09:14,686 --> 00:09:18,238
Tell the committee, Pilgrim's landed
on Plymouth rock.

99
00:09:18,943 --> 00:09:21,248
The Mars samples are safe and sound.

100
00:09:21,575 --> 00:09:24,450
Copy that, Commander.
Congratulations.

101
00:09:32,957 --> 00:09:37,300
ISS , DAY 2

102
00:10:05,143 --> 00:10:05,896
Ready?

103
00:10:07,384 --> 00:10:11,066
We are recording.
06:40 UTC.

104
00:10:14,153 --> 00:10:16,868
Pilgrim wasn't just seeing
things in the Mars soil.

105
00:10:17,467 --> 00:10:21,250
We are looking at a large
single cell. Inert.

106
00:10:21,793 --> 00:10:23,321
Unmistakably biological.

107
00:10:23,589 --> 00:10:27,363
And like organisms on Earth,
it has what appears to be a nucleus...

108
00:10:27,788 --> 00:10:29,056
cytoplasm.

109
00:10:31,190 --> 00:10:32,782
The cell wall is thick.

110
00:10:34,311 --> 00:10:38,110
Those hairs look like cilia. Longer
than we're used to seeing on Earth.

111
00:10:38,550 --> 00:10:39,933
More like flagella.

112
00:10:43,733 --> 00:10:46,066
- What's the temperature?
- Minus 110.

113
00:10:47,004 --> 00:10:48,179
Start bringing it up.

114
00:10:48,852 --> 00:10:49,926
Minus 82.

115
00:10:52,464 --> 00:10:53,328
Even quicker.

116
00:10:56,576 --> 00:10:57,930
Minus 55.

117
00:11:00,027 --> 00:11:02,024
This is some Re-Animator shit.

118
00:11:02,289 --> 00:11:04,247
Oh, that's a very obscure reference.

119
00:11:04,447 --> 00:11:06,594
- Not for a nerd.
- Not if you're a nerd.

120
00:11:07,370 --> 00:11:08,715
Minus 17.

121
00:11:10,177 --> 00:11:13,070
- Zero degrees.
- Oh, come on...

122
00:11:13,245 --> 00:11:14,328
18

123
00:11:15,146 --> 00:11:16,137
19

124
00:11:17,162 --> 00:11:19,145
20 degrees Celsius.

125
00:11:23,379 --> 00:11:24,465
Nothing.

126
00:11:28,671 --> 00:11:29,866
Update?

127
00:11:33,408 --> 00:11:35,421
I'm gonna try a different atmosphere.

128
00:11:36,574 --> 00:11:39,627
Closer to Proterozoic Earth
than today's Mars.

129
00:11:40,799 --> 00:11:42,298
Less oxygen

130
00:11:42,808 --> 00:11:44,665
more carbon dioxide.

131
00:11:45,293 --> 00:11:47,270
And then, when Sho is ready,

132
00:11:48,463 --> 00:11:49,730
a growth medium.

133
00:11:50,595 --> 00:11:52,653
In this case, glucose.

134
00:11:57,158 --> 00:11:58,395
Come on, dance.

135
00:12:21,689 --> 00:12:22,790
Did it just move?

136
00:12:24,239 --> 00:12:25,297
Hugh?

137
00:12:34,823 --> 00:12:36,118
My God.

138
00:12:37,793 --> 00:12:40,780
It worked. You woke it.

139
00:12:44,181 --> 00:12:48,640
We are looking at the first incontrovertible
proof of life beyond Earth.

140
00:12:55,357 --> 00:12:57,777
Oh, it's beautiful.

141
00:13:13,200 --> 00:13:15,943
Our first question is from Allen.
He is 9 years old.

142
00:13:15,976 --> 00:13:18,684
And Allen, what is your question
for Commander Golovkina?

143
00:13:18,963 --> 00:13:19,963
Is it green?

144
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:24,221
It's not visible at all actually,
it's microscopic. A single cell.

145
00:13:24,585 --> 00:13:27,567
To put it into perspective,
your body is made up of trillions.

146
00:13:27,992 --> 00:13:30,246
There is an alien inside of me?

147
00:13:30,964 --> 00:13:33,226
Oh, I bet your teacher
sometimes thinks so.

148
00:13:33,429 --> 00:13:35,926
Well, we have another question
from 8 year old Marrie.

149
00:13:36,062 --> 00:13:38,153
Are you bringing the alien back to Earth?

150
00:13:38,287 --> 00:13:41,233
No, we are gonna keep it up here.
We're gonna study it where it's safe.

151
00:13:42,019 --> 00:13:42,913
Commander?

152
00:13:43,151 --> 00:13:46,665
The ISS is the most
expensive house ever built.

153
00:13:46,870 --> 00:13:51,701
200 billion American dollars and counting
because we keep adding rooms.

154
00:13:52,069 --> 00:13:55,238
It flies around Earth 16 times a day.

155
00:13:55,912 --> 00:13:58,969
In 30 years, over one hundred
people have lived here

156
00:13:59,712 --> 00:14:02,934
thanks to generous countries
like Russia, the USA and China.

157
00:14:03,127 --> 00:14:04,900
We call this Tranquility node.

158
00:14:05,511 --> 00:14:09,420
Dr Jordan here is about to break the
record for consecutive days on space.

159
00:14:09,839 --> 00:14:12,489
473 straight.

160
00:14:12,727 --> 00:14:14,650
Your mommy must miss you.

161
00:14:16,389 --> 00:14:20,136
She does, but I have family here.

162
00:14:20,330 --> 00:14:23,812
Yeah, the kind that teases him
'cause his mommy misses him.

163
00:14:23,933 --> 00:14:27,074
This is one of our newest crew members,
Miranda North

164
00:14:27,276 --> 00:14:30,112
From the Centers for Disease Control.
She is a doctor too.

165
00:14:30,322 --> 00:14:32,559
You guys are witnessing a space triathlon.

166
00:14:32,845 --> 00:14:34,038
Yeah, I'm winning.

167
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:37,064
We have another question
from 7 year old Layla.

168
00:14:37,146 --> 00:14:39,736
We think this question
is a little personal.

169
00:14:39,874 --> 00:14:42,912
- Go ahead.
- How do you go to the bathroom?

170
00:14:43,610 --> 00:14:44,599
Funnel.

171
00:14:45,675 --> 00:14:46,775
Tube.

172
00:14:47,738 --> 00:14:49,178
And some suction.

173
00:14:50,285 --> 00:14:54,615
Everything we do on the ISS
is like everything you do on Earth.

174
00:14:54,995 --> 00:14:58,171
- Except different. You like peas?
- Yeah.

175
00:15:03,624 --> 00:15:06,090
Mmh.. boom!

176
00:15:06,906 --> 00:15:09,024
So, have you got a name yet?

177
00:15:09,485 --> 00:15:12,150
For that we wanna go down live,
just outside our studio in Time Square

178
00:15:12,186 --> 00:15:13,993
to one very brave class mate.

179
00:15:14,148 --> 00:15:16,666
- Hi, Dominique.
- Hi.

180
00:15:17,093 --> 00:15:19,795
Calvin Coolidge Elementary

181
00:15:19,948 --> 00:15:23,831
was chosen among 11.000 schools

182
00:15:24,129 --> 00:15:25,717
for this honor.

183
00:15:26,734 --> 00:15:29,055
We name the Martian

184
00:15:29,135 --> 00:15:31,619
after our school.

185
00:15:32,709 --> 00:15:34,138
"Calvin"

186
00:15:41,521 --> 00:15:44,058
Control? Control?

187
00:15:44,138 --> 00:15:47,178
If you're listening, and you probably are
'cause you're creepy that way

188
00:15:47,301 --> 00:15:49,838
Can I just rant for a second
about the micromanagement?

189
00:15:50,004 --> 00:15:51,568
We are not blood filled new puppets.

190
00:15:51,705 --> 00:15:54,426
I come from a long line of plumbers.
I can fix a shower.

191
00:15:54,980 --> 00:15:58,243
If I can't, well, Hugh doesn't
shower anyway. He's British.

192
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,061
- First international incident.
- Oh, I think, better cool you.

193
00:16:01,086 --> 00:16:03,044
You're just a very underpaid
nation, everybody knows it.

194
00:16:03,079 --> 00:16:07,140
- Don't be an ugly American.
- Wow, who's ugly now?

195
00:16:07,387 --> 00:16:10,771
I nailed Step 17. I am a very
good looking American, you know.

196
00:16:10,838 --> 00:16:12,012
Oh, I see, it's right there.

197
00:16:12,079 --> 00:16:14,477
- Control, I'm sorry about him.
- Control can't hear you.

198
00:16:14,502 --> 00:16:15,807
I don't know where rage
like that comes from.

199
00:16:15,832 --> 00:16:17,767
Can you please just fix the shower?

200
00:16:18,108 --> 00:16:20,498
You know, I can...
I can do this myself.

201
00:16:20,537 --> 00:16:22,483
No, I know. You just haven't.

202
00:16:24,546 --> 00:16:27,429
Doctors are always so bad at looking
after themselves, aren't they?

203
00:16:34,155 --> 00:16:38,402
You've spent too much time up here, David.
Your atrophy has accelerated.

204
00:16:39,318 --> 00:16:41,302
And you're close to radiation limit.

205
00:16:42,895 --> 00:16:46,298
You noticed any...
glowing in the dark?

206
00:16:53,320 --> 00:16:54,702
I like it up here.

207
00:16:57,988 --> 00:17:00,614
When I was in the military, we go to Syria.

208
00:17:00,852 --> 00:17:01,507
You know

209
00:17:03,985 --> 00:17:05,689
We set up a hospital

210
00:17:06,530 --> 00:17:08,725
Treat all the casualties, and then

211
00:17:09,186 --> 00:17:13,195
We come back... a week
or two later, and the

212
00:17:13,619 --> 00:17:15,831
whole village would
just be bombed out.

213
00:17:20,975 --> 00:17:23,634
That Earth isn't all
Syria though, is it?

214
00:17:28,755 --> 00:17:31,096
Can't stand what we do
to each other down there.

215
00:17:32,943 --> 00:17:36,129
But it's home, and it's beautiful.

216
00:17:36,843 --> 00:17:38,500
Don't you miss anything?

217
00:17:39,418 --> 00:17:43,761
Don't you miss fresh water, or birds

218
00:17:44,417 --> 00:17:45,676
the trees?

219
00:17:48,379 --> 00:17:51,831
I like the hum up here.

220
00:17:56,424 --> 00:17:58,280
And the air.

221
00:18:18,243 --> 00:18:21,482
What you've achieved with
Calvin is remarkable, Hugh.

222
00:18:23,152 --> 00:18:25,311
I haven't had a chance to say that.

223
00:18:25,765 --> 00:18:29,851
Thank you. I think these will prove
to be greatest days of my life.

224
00:18:32,062 --> 00:18:35,700
I wish I had a time machine,
so I could visit the 10 year old me.

225
00:18:37,103 --> 00:18:38,632
Did he want to be an astronaut?

226
00:18:39,562 --> 00:18:41,506
He wanted out of the wheelchair.

227
00:18:43,783 --> 00:18:45,627
And how does he feel now?

228
00:18:47,853 --> 00:18:49,132
Like a bird.

229
00:18:56,048 --> 00:18:59,724
Oh, don't get me wrong.
I don't hate the chair.

230
00:19:00,289 --> 00:19:01,303
I love it.

231
00:19:03,182 --> 00:19:05,650
For the sake of the anonymous
genius who invented it.

232
00:19:08,660 --> 00:19:10,390
You're one of a kind, Hugh.

233
00:19:12,723 --> 00:19:13,527
Goodnight.

234
00:19:34,135 --> 00:19:35,648
ISS, DAY 12

235
00:19:35,972 --> 00:19:39,555
- 11:20 UTC.
- Hold on to your helmets.

236
00:19:39,670 --> 00:19:41,735
The glucose intake is very rapid.

237
00:19:42,728 --> 00:19:46,245
The specimen's cells have began
to move together on mass, as a unit.

238
00:19:47,431 --> 00:19:50,158
And they've also shown
electrical activity

239
00:19:50,303 --> 00:19:53,037
in what resembles
a growing neural network.

240
00:19:53,796 --> 00:19:55,494
Notice I didn't say "brain".

241
00:19:56,212 --> 00:19:57,683
Dr North speaking.

242
00:19:57,936 --> 00:20:00,149
It's our educated opinion
that this new species

243
00:20:00,175 --> 00:20:03,366
is not a colony of fast dividing
single celled organisms

244
00:20:03,492 --> 00:20:06,721
but one larger solitary
organism composed of

245
00:20:06,921 --> 00:20:09,450
trillions of
identical cooperative cells.

246
00:20:10,105 --> 00:20:13,885
Here's what's fascinating...
I mean, it's all fascinating, isn't it?

247
00:20:15,154 --> 00:20:17,374
Unlike most multi-cellular organisms

248
00:20:17,526 --> 00:20:21,370
every cell of Calvin's can perform
every somatic function on its own.

249
00:20:22,140 --> 00:20:25,849
Every single cell is simultaneously

250
00:20:26,001 --> 00:20:30,934
a muscle cell and a nerve cell,
and a photo-receptive cell.

251
